drills.explanation:
Copy only the path that changes (`state` → `items` → the one line) and return `state` itself for no-ops:

```js
case 'setQty': {
  if (!has) return state;
  if (action.qty <= 0) return { ...state, items: state.items.filter((i) => i.sku !== action.sku) };
  const line = state.items.find((i) => i.sku === action.sku);
  if (line.qty === action.qty) return state;
  return { ...state, items: state.items.map((i) => (i.sku === action.sku ? { ...i, qty: action.qty } : i)) };
}
```

The frozen inputs make any mutation throw (frozen objects reject writes in the module's strict code), and the identity checks enforce the bail-out that lets React skip re-rendering.

Three wrong turns and the tests that catch them. Copying every line in `add`, not only the matched one, gives a correct-looking cart, but "unchanged lines keep their identity" fails, and memoised rows would all re-render. Dropping the `line.qty === action.qty` check returns a new object for a no-op, so "no-op actions return the same state object" fails. Clearing without the empty check fails "clear empties, and clearing an empty cart is a no-op (boundary)".

